  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nActive weather to continue overnight with the potential for heavy\nrain and flooding. A wavy surface frontal boundary is bisecting\nILN/s area. Forcing will increase late in the afternoon into this \nevening as a short wave induces a wave of low pressure along the \nboundary. A modest 20-25 kt low level jet will accompany this wave. \nPWAT values remain anomalously high around 2 inches and the warm \ncloud layer will be deep. This will lead to the potential for heavy \nrain and flooding along and north of the front. HREF is showing the \npotential for 1 to 3 inches stripes of rainfall overnight with LPMM \nindicating that local totals in excess of 4 inches will be possible. \nThe exact placement of the heavy rain axis is somewhat uncertain \nwith the most favored location along the southern periphery of the \nI-corridor. Have issued a flood watch for potential flash flooding \novernight and added a buffer of counties north and south due to the \nuncertainty of exact placement. \n \n\nAlong with the flood threat a threat for severe weather exists into\nthis evening. Moderate instability combined with favorable DCAPE\nvalues may lead to a conditional severe weather threat with some of\nthe initial storms late this afternoon into this evening. The main\nthreat is damaging winds. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nCooler airmass will settle across the region. A deep mid level trough\ndigging southeast from the Upper MS Valley will close off as it \nmoves across the area midweek. At the surface, an inverted trough \nwill extend along the western slopes of the Appalachians. This \ncombination will result in showery conditions, particularly across \neastern counties. For the period Tuesday thru Friday - high\ntemperatures look to range from the mid/upper 60s north to the lower \n70s south. These readings will be close to 10 degrees below normal.",
